blocks.text:
&ldquo;Mars Tom, I reckon dey&rsquo;s a mistake about it somers&mdash;dey
                mos&rsquo; sholy is. I&rsquo;s religious myself, en I knows plenty religious
                people, but I hain&rsquo;t run across none dat acts like dat.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, Mars Tom, <i>don&rsquo;t!</i> Ef you teches him, we&rsquo;s
                gone&mdash;we&rsquo;s gone sho&rsquo;! I ain&rsquo;t gwine anear him, not for
                nothin&rsquo; in dis worl&rsquo;. Mars Tom, he&rsquo;s plumb crazy.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It&rsquo;s got everything to do with it. Illinois is green, Indiana is
                pink. You show me any pink down here, if you can. No, sir; it&rsquo;s
                green.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, if I was such a numbskull as you, Huck Finn, I would jump over.
                Seen it on the map! Huck Finn, did you reckon the States was the same color
                out-of-doors as they are on the map?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, the thing that&rsquo;s happened is that this old bladder has slid
                over Illinois and Indiana and Ohio like nothing, and this is the east end of
                Pennsylvania or New York, or somewheres around there.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I do, and it&rsquo;s dead sure. We&rsquo;ve covered about fifteen
                degrees of longitude since we left St. Louis yesterday afternoon, and them
                clocks are right. We&rsquo;ve come close on to eight hundred miles.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It grieves me to hear you talk like dat, Mars Tom; I&rsquo;s right down
                ashamed to hear you talk like dat, arter de way you&rsquo;s been raised.
                Yassir, it&rsquo;d break yo&rsquo; Aunt Polly&rsquo;s heart to hear you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Scriminate! I never heard such ignorance. There ain&rsquo;t no
                discriminating about it. When he makes you and some more of his children black,
                and makes the rest of us white, what do you call that?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Atlantic Ocean&mdash;Atlantic. Land, don&rsquo;t it sound great! And
                that&rsquo;s <i>it</i>&mdash;and <i>we</i> are looking at it&mdash;we! Why,
                it&rsquo;s just too splendid to believe!&rdquo;

&ldquo;They don&rsquo;t want to go to England. All right, I&rsquo;ll change the
                course. They want to leave me. I know they do. Well, they shall&mdash;and
                <i>now!</i>&rdquo;

Then we heard a couple of terrible screams, and then another not so loud, and
                then another that was &rsquo;way below, and you could only <i>just</i> hear it;
                and I heard Jim say, &ldquo;Po&rsquo; Mars Tom!&rdquo;

&ldquo;I dasn&rsquo;t, Huck. I knowed somebody plunged down past me, but I
                didn&rsquo;t know who it was in the dark. It could &rsquo;a&rsquo; been you, it
                could &rsquo;a&rsquo; been Jim.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You know what that means, easy enough. It means that somebody has got to
                stay on watch and steer this thing the same as he would a ship, or she&rsquo;ll
                wander around and go wherever the wind wants her to.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Wandering,&rdquo; he says, kinder troubled&mdash;&ldquo;wandering,
                without any doubt. She&rsquo;s in a wind now that&rsquo;s blowing her south of
                east. We don&rsquo;t know how long that&rsquo;s been going on, either.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know, and nobody don&rsquo;t know. It&rsquo;s just a word,
                and it&rsquo;s a mighty good word, too. There ain&rsquo;t many that lays over
                it. I don&rsquo;t believe there&rsquo;s <i>any</i> that does.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know what it means, I tell you. It&rsquo;s a word that
                people uses for&mdash;for&mdash;well, it&rsquo;s ornamental. They don&rsquo;t
                put ruffles on a shirt to keep a person warm, do they?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, Mars Tom, sholy you can&rsquo;t mean to say I don&rsquo;t know
                about shirts, when, goodness knows, I&rsquo;s toted home de washin&rsquo; ever
                sence&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, it&rsquo;s all right, then; and we&rsquo;ll let bygones be
                bygones. I don&rsquo;t know for certain what a welkin is, but when we land in
                London we&rsquo;ll make it ring, anyway, and don&rsquo;t you forget it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Run! Run fo&rsquo; yo&rsquo; life! Hit&rsquo;s a lion; I kin see him
                thoo de glass! Run, boys; do please heel it de bes&rsquo; you kin. He&rsquo;s
                bu&rsquo;sted outen de menagerie, en dey ain&rsquo;t nobody to stop him!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now you begin to see, don&rsquo;t you, that <i>distance</i> ain&rsquo;t
                the thing to judge by, at all; it&rsquo;s the time it takes to go the distance
                <i>in</i> that <i>counts</i>, ain&rsquo;t it?&rdquo;

&ldquo;My lan&rsquo;, Mars Tom, I never knowed dey was so much <i>to</i> de
                beas&rsquo;. No, sir, I never had no idea of it, and dat&rsquo;s de
                fac&rsquo;.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Some people can see, and some can&rsquo;t&mdash;just as that man said.
                Let alone a camel, if a cyclone had gone by, <i>you</i> duffers wouldn&rsquo;t
                &rsquo;a&rsquo; noticed the track.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know nothing of the kind. There used to be forty thousand
                million people that seen the sun move from one side of the sky to the other
                every day. Did that prove that the sun <i>done</i> it?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Course it did. En besides, dey warn&rsquo;t no &rsquo;casion to prove
                it. A body &rsquo;at&rsquo;s got any sense ain&rsquo;t gwine to doubt it. Dah
                she is now&mdash;a sailin&rsquo; thoo de sky, like she allays done.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; he says, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m lost in the sky with no company
                but a passel of low-down animals that don&rsquo;t know no more than the head
                boss of a university did three or four hundred years ago.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, my goodness, oh, my goodness gracious, dah&rsquo;s de lake
                agi&rsquo;n!&rdquo; yelled Jim, just then. &ldquo;<i>Now</i>, Mars Tom, what
                you gwine to say?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ghost, you gander! It ain&rsquo;t anything but air and heat and
                thirstiness pasted together by a person&rsquo;s imagination. If I&mdash;gimme
                the glass!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now lay on your back and float till you&rsquo;re rested and got your
                pluck back, then I&rsquo;ll dip the ladder in the water and you can climb
                aboard.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, hit stan&rsquo; to reason we is. You knows how long we&rsquo;s
                been a-skimmin&rsquo; over it. Mus&rsquo; be mos&rsquo; out o&rsquo;
                san&rsquo;. Hit&rsquo;s a wonder to me dat it&rsquo;s hilt out as long as it
                has.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, go &rsquo;long! we ain&rsquo;t much more than fairly <i>started</i>
                across this Desert yet. The United States is a pretty big country, ain&rsquo;t
                it? Ain&rsquo;t it, Huck?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; I says, &ldquo;it clean beats me. Why, Tom, it shows that
                the Lord took as much pains makin&rsquo; this Desert as makin&rsquo; the United
                States and all them other countries.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Mars Tom, I b&rsquo;lieve it uz jes like when you&rsquo;s buildin&rsquo;
                a house; dey&rsquo;s allays a lot o&rsquo; truck en rubbish lef&rsquo; over.
                What does you do wid it? Doan&rsquo; you take en k&rsquo;yart it off en dump it
                into a ole vacant back lot? &rsquo;Course. Now, den, it&rsquo;s my opinion hit
                was jes like dat&mdash;dat de Great Sahara warn&rsquo;t made at all, she jes
                <i>happen</i>&rsquo;.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That&rsquo;s it&mdash;it&rsquo;s the one I&rsquo;ve been looking for,
                sure. If I&rsquo;m right, it&rsquo;s the one the dervish took the man into and
                showed him all the treasures.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You ain&rsquo;t in society, you know, and you don&rsquo;t really need
                all you&rsquo;ve got. Won&rsquo;t you be good, and let me have ten of your
                camels?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I wasn&rsquo;t keeping anything back from you. I don&rsquo;t mind
                telling you what would happen if I put it on. You&rsquo;d never see again.
                You&rsquo;d be stone-blind the rest of your days.&rdquo;

Tom allowed we had twenty tons of it, and wondered what we better do with it;
                it was good sand, and it didn&rsquo;t seem good sense to throw it away. Jim
                says:

&ldquo;Well, sah, she&rsquo;s wuth a quarter of a dollar a load at home, en I
                reckon we&rsquo;s got as much as twenty loads, hain&rsquo;t we? How much would
                dat be?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, ef dat ain&rsquo;t makin&rsquo; money de easiest ever <i>I</i>
                struck! She jes&rsquo; rained in&mdash;never cos&rsquo; us a lick o&rsquo;
                work. Le&rsquo;s mosey right along, Mars Tom.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I don&rsquo;t know yet. It&rsquo;s got to be ciphered, and it
                ain&rsquo;t the easiest job to do, either, because it&rsquo;s over four million
                square miles of sand at ten cents a vial.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Mars Tom, we can&rsquo;t &rsquo;ford all dem vials&mdash;a king
                couldn&rsquo;t. We better not try to take de whole Desert, Mars Tom, de vials
                gwyne to bust us, sho&rsquo;.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, they can&rsquo;t raise Sahara sand in America, of course, and when
                they can&rsquo;t raise a thing there, the duty is fourteen hundred thousand per
                cent. on it if you try to fetch it in from where they do raise it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Who said there <i>was?</i> What do you talk to me like that for, Huck
                Finn? You wait till I say a thing&rsquo;s got sense in it before you go to
                accusing me of saying it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Mars Tom, do dey jam dat duty onto everything we can&rsquo;t raise in
                America, en don&rsquo;t make no &rsquo;stinction &rsquo;twix&rsquo;
                anything?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yassir! you&rsquo;s jes&rsquo; right, &rsquo;deed you is, honey&mdash;it
                come from heaven, en dat&rsquo;s a foreign country. <i>Now</i>, den! do dey put
                a tax on dat blessin&rsquo;?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Course I&rsquo;s de stronges&rsquo;, en I&rsquo;s willin&rsquo; to do a
                share accordin&rsquo;, but by jings you&rsquo;s kinder pilin&rsquo; it onto ole
                Jim, Mars Tom, hain&rsquo;t you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;You&rsquo;ll see that they&rsquo;ll have to apologize for insulting the
                flag, and pay an indemnity, too, on top of it even if they git off <i>that</i>
                easy.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, they&rsquo;re not. One is a balloon and the other&rsquo;s a horse.
                It&rsquo;s very different. Next you&rsquo;ll be saying a house and a cow is the
                same thing.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Tom, it ain&rsquo;t no use. Principles is all very well, but they
                don&rsquo;t git around that one big fact, that the thing that a balloon can do
                ain&rsquo;t no sort of proof of what a horse can do.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Shucks, Huck, you don&rsquo;t get the idea at all. Now look here a
                minute&mdash;it&rsquo;s perfectly plain. Don&rsquo;t we fly through the
                air?&rdquo;

&ldquo;As I understand it, the whole thing is in the buttons and the
                peg&mdash;the rest ain&rsquo;t of no consequence. A button is one shape, a peg
                is another shape, but that ain&rsquo;t any matter?&rdquo;

&ldquo;My lan&rsquo;, ef Huck ain&rsquo;t got him ag&rsquo;in! Huck&rsquo;s
                landed him high en dry dis time, I tell you! Hit&rsquo;s de smartes&rsquo; trap
                I ever see a body walk inter&mdash;en ef I&mdash;&rdquo;
